<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>A US distribution agreement should say clearly what the distributor may sell, where and to whom, on what terms, what each side commits to, who owns what, and how the arrangement ends. The clauses that cause the most trouble later are exclusivity, minimum commitments, pricing, and termination. Agree on those deliberately, and have a US lawyer review the final draft. This post is general guidance, not legal advice.</p>

<h2 id="scope">Products, territory and customers</h2>
<p>Start with scope. List the products covered, and say how new products are added. Define the territory precisely: the whole US, named states or regions, or specific customer segments. If you plan to sell some accounts yourself or through other partners, name them or describe them, so there is no argument later about who owns a customer.</p>
<p>Online sales deserve their own sentence. A territory defined by geography can blur when a distributor sells through a website or a marketplace that reaches the whole country.</p>

<h2 id="exclusivity">Exclusivity</h2>
<p>Exclusivity is the clause distributors ask for most often and the one vendors regret most often. If you grant it, tie it to performance: minimum purchases or sales over a defined period, with exclusivity ending or narrowing if they are not met. Consider granting it for a segment or region rather than the whole country, and for a first term rather than indefinitely. If you do not grant it, say so explicitly.</p>

<h2 id="commitments">What each side commits to</h2>
<p>A good agreement records the work, not just the rights. On the distributor's side this can include minimum purchase or stock levels, marketing activity, trained sales and technical staff, sales and inventory reporting, and customer support duties. On your side it can include training, marketing support or funds, lead sharing, product samples, technical escalation and response times.</p>
<p>Commitments written as specific actions with dates are far easier to manage than general promises to "use best efforts." If a commitment matters to you, make it measurable.</p>

<h2 id="pricing-and-payment">Pricing, payment and delivery</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pricing.</strong> How the distributor's price is set, how and with what notice it can change, and how price protection works if you lower prices while they hold stock.</li>
  <li><strong>Resale price.</strong> In the US, attempts to fix the price a distributor charges its customers can raise antitrust concerns. Suggested retail prices are common, but have a lawyer review any clause that controls resale pricing.</li>
  <li><strong>Payment terms.</strong> Currency, due dates, late payment consequences and any credit limit.</li>
  <li><strong>Delivery terms.</strong> Who arranges shipping, where risk passes, who acts as importer of record, and who pays duties and tariffs. Tariff changes can shift costs quickly, so decide in advance who bears them.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="brand-and-ip">Brand, intellectual property and data</h2>
<p>State that you own your trademarks, product designs and software, and grant the distributor a limited right to use your brand to sell the products, under your guidelines. Say who owns customer data and lists developed during the agreement, and what happens to them at the end. If the distributor will register your trademark or product approvals locally, specify that it does so in your name.</p>

<h2 id="warranties-and-liability">Warranties, returns and liability</h2>
<p>Set out the product warranty you give, how warranty claims flow from customer to distributor to you, and how returns of unsold or damaged stock are handled. Address product liability and recalls, insurance each side must carry, and the limits on what either side can claim from the other. Regulated products may also need clauses on compliance, labeling and record-keeping.</p>

<h2 id="term-and-termination">Term and termination</h2>
<p>Most disputes happen at the end of a relationship, so this section deserves the most care.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Term.</strong> A first term with renewal by agreement gives both sides a natural review point.</li>
  <li><strong>Termination for cause.</strong> Breach, insolvency and missed minimums, with a period to fix the problem where appropriate.</li>
  <li><strong>Termination for convenience.</strong> Whether either side can end the agreement without cause, and with how much notice.</li>
  <li><strong>After termination.</strong> What happens to remaining stock, open orders, customer data, marketing materials and the use of your brand.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some US states have laws that protect certain types of dealers and distributors and restrict how agreements with them can be ended. Whether they apply depends on the industry and the relationship, which is one of the main reasons to have the agreement checked by a lawyer licensed in the relevant state.</p>

<h2 id="disputes">Governing law and disputes</h2>
<p>Choose which state's law governs the agreement and how disputes are resolved: in court, or through arbitration, and where. International vendors sometimes prefer arbitration because awards can be easier to enforce across borders. Whatever you choose, make sure it is workable for both sides.</p>

<h2 id="reporting-and-audit">Reporting and review</h2>
<p>Ask for regular sales and inventory reports in a set format, and the right to review the distributor's records on your products if needed. These reports tell you where your product is selling and whether minimums are being met, and they make renewal conversations far easier.</p>

<h2 id="before-you-sign">Before you sign</h2>
<p>Read the agreement as if the relationship had gone badly and you were trying to leave it. If that path is unclear or costly, renegotiate now, while both sides are still enthusiastic. And have a US lawyer review it before signing. The cost of that review is small next to the cost of a dispute.</p>
